---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('mailchimp')!;
const b = appBySlug('aftership-email')!;
const path = '/compare/mailchimp-vs-aftership-email';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/mailchimp-vs-aftership-email');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/mailchimp-vs-aftership-email');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does AfterShip Tracking replace abandoned-checkout marketing?",
    "answer": "No. Tracking notification workflows and AfterShip Email recovery flows have different triggers and must be configured separately."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Mailchimp remain the marketing sender?",
    "answer": "Yes. Evaluate keeping Mailchimp recovery while AfterShip Tracking handles shipment updates, with explicit message ownership."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should shipment problems trigger a discount?",
    "answer": "Route unresolved delivery exceptions to support before scheduling another promotional offer."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are these recovery results measured?",
    "answer": "No. The proposed acceptance cases and fixed-goods-cost examples are planning tools, not observed vendor performance."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Separate recovery marketing from delivery notifications.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Mailchimp", "reason": "Retain a working audience and store-connected recovery."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "AfterShip Email", "reason": "Assign marketing and notification responsibilities explicitly."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Choose a marketing owner before adding another AfterShip product</h2>
<p>A store can use Mailchimp for newsletters and checkout reminders while AfterShip Tracking sends shipment updates. Buying tracking software does not by itself establish a new cart-recovery sender. Evaluate AfterShip Email when you want to move the marketing workflow itself and can maintain its eligibility rules, templates and exits. Keep Mailchimp when that workflow already works and shipment communication is the only missing job.</p>
<h2>What the documented workflows actually do</h2>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/create-abandoned-cart-customer-journey/">Mailchimp documents abandoned-cart marketing flows</a> with cart content, a return button and wait settings for a connected store. It supports more than list broadcasts. Verify the required automation allowance in your account rather than treating an old entry price as a quote for the complete recovery program.</p>
<p><a href="https://support.aftership.com/en/email/articles/15440875-how-to-increase-conversions-with-abandoned-checkout-emails">AfterShip Email’s Shopify checkout guide</a> places recovery in Emails and SMS → Flows and describes minimum checkout value, recent-order filters and delays. These are useful controls to inspect during evaluation; a recent-order rule must match the period and comparison you intended.</p>
<p><a href="https://support.aftership.com/en/tracking/articles/15441679-how-to-create-custom-email-workflows">AfterShip Tracking’s notification guide</a> instead describes delivery-status workflows, including exceptions and failed attempts, under Notifications → Emails and SMS. Its listed plan requirements concern Tracking workflows, not a promise of bundled Email access. Check both subscriptions separately. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Map messages by purpose</h2>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Customer state</th><th>Proposed owner and action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Unfinished checkout</td><td>One marketing sender: Mailchimp or AfterShip Email; return to the current checkout.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Paid order awaiting shipment</td><td>Assigned order-notification system; cancel the checkout reminder.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carrier exception or failed delivery</td><td>Tracking notification plus a support owner; pause discretionary promotion while resolving the issue.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delivered order with a return request</td><td>Support handles the request; any later marketing follows its own eligibility decision.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>This is an operating design, not a claim that a shared brand automatically synchronizes every suppression. Record the checkout and order IDs, message purpose, sender and queued send time. If delivery information cannot reach the marketing tool reliably, use a supported connection or a staff-controlled segment before relying on it to suppress offers.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance cases across the purchase boundary</h2>
<p>Start an eligible checkout, then purchase during the delay. Inspect the marketing queue and the order-notification history: the reminder should stop and the intended receipt should appear once. Replay the order event and confirm it does not create another reminder. Next create a delivery exception for a previous order while the same customer has a new cart. Decide whether support should resolve that exception before another offer; verify the rule in the chosen sender rather than assuming Tracking enforces it.</p>
<p>Test an unsubscribed buyer’s shipment update separately from eligibility for promotional recovery. A delivery address or tracking event is not evidence of marketing permission. Before using SMS, verify channel permission, local send windows and total cross-channel pressure. Choose a delay and cap for a small pilot; the former 120-minute/46-hour/5-hour sequence is not a measured optimum. These are proposed checks, not tests we ran.</p>
<h2>Useful assistance before an incentive</h2>
<p>For international shipping shock, show the actual shipping and delivery terms; do not promise a date from an old shipment. A gift shopper needs the gift-message and sizing path restored. A mobile visitor needs a usable checkout link. A returning buyer may have a support issue that a generic reminder misses. For a size matrix with unavailable variants, rebuild the available basket or suppress the offer instead of linking to a fully out-of-stock cart.</p>
<p>A bundle missing its advertised reduction calls for checking eligibility and coupon stacking. A subscription toggle should restore the intended purchase type. A wholesale basket below MOQ needs the threshold or quotation route. BNPL or financing abandonment calls for accurate payment options, never an assurance of approval. On cancellation, partial refund or an open return, reassess promotional eligibility and queued reminders against the actual order state.</p>
<h2>Keep discount costs visible</h2>
<p>Hypothetical fixed-goods-cost examples: a $708 basket at 35% gross margin gives $247.80 before other expenses. A 20% discount costs $141.60 and leaves $106.20. A $371 basket at the same margin gives $129.85; a 13% discount costs $48.23 and leaves $81.62. These figures exclude shipping subsidy, fees, returns and app costs. Neither basket proves a conversion lift or a safe universal incentive cap.</p>
<h2>Cut over marketing without disrupting tracking</h2>
<p>Export active recovery steps and pending sends, preserve unsubscribe evidence and label which tool owns each message. Rebuild the candidate marketing flow in a non-sending state. Pause the old recovery flow and reconcile queued reminders before enabling the replacement; do not disable shipment notifications merely to avoid marketing duplicates. Restore the previous marketing owner if a live eligible checkout fails enrollment, a purchase fails suppression or the return link loses the basket.</p>
<p>Compare eligible checkouts, delivered reminders, completed orders and gross profit after incentives using the same reporting window. If volume supports a randomized no-reminder group, predefine its size and evaluation period; platform-attributed revenue alone cannot demonstrate additional orders. Include separate Tracking and Email charges and staff effort in the decision.</p>

</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
